{"id":"basic_scatter_example","compound_name":"Graphs::Scatter::basic_scatter_example","source_path":"basic_scatter_example/basic_scatter_example.json","category":"Scatter Pack","author":"Autodesk","description":"Creates an effect of layered instances by scattering with blue noise and then angling the instanced geometries upward so they lay over each other. ","documentation_file":"basic_scatter_example_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"Creates an effect of layered instances by scattering with blue noise and then angling the instanced geometries upward so they lay over each other. \n\n## Scattering and orientations\n\nThis graph scatters onto a mesh, which also generates orientations. \n\nThe output of scatter_points is a points object. Points is a kind of geometry representing points, and is the basis for other kinds of geometry in Bifrost, such as instances, meshes, and strands. Any operation that works on points will also work on instances, meshes and strands. \n\nUsually scattered points use normals and UVs to orient instances on a mesh. The Y vector points along the normal, and the positive Z vector points down a tangent derived from UVs. If no UVs are available as in this case, it can use the structure of the mesh itself, as each face has an implicit orientation based on the order of its vertices. Regular parametric geometry such as mesh spheres or toruses contain a built-in structure from which we can start with a very regular flow of orientations. \n\n## Create instances\n\nCreate instances puts instances on points. When no instance geometry is provided to \"create instances\", the default instanced geometry is a colorful sphere. \n\nWe are using that default geometry in this example. \n\n## Scale points, rotate points, randomize point rotation\n\nScale points is being used to scale each point by the same value. It can also be used to scale the points by varying per-point values, but here’s being used with a single value. The scale and weights ports here are “interpreted auto ports”, meaning they can be driven by a variety of kinds of data. These include singles, per-point arrays of data, fields, and strings which refer to properties. \n\nRotate points is similar – it rotates the point’s existing orientation, or creates a new orientation if none exists. The input ports are also “interpreted auto” ports allowing more advanced usage. In this example each point is being rotated by the same value, in this case in local space. \n\nYou can also use \"type wrangling\", by right clicking on the ports and setting value types and avoid the need for the value nodes.\n\nRandomize point rotation is like rotate points but applies a random rotation to each point.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.15.0.9","bif":"2.15.0.9","fields":"2.15.0.9","scatter":"2.15.0.9"},"graph_ports":[{"portName":"out_points","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"}],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::proxy","enabled":"true"}],"counts":{"nodes":36,"connections":44,"values":51,"compounds":5},"node_types":{"Value::Math::float3":6,"Value::float":2,"Modeling::Primitive::create_mesh_sphere":2,"Diagnostic::Display::assign_diagnostic_material":2,"Modeling::Points::scatter_points":1,"dummy_geomery":1,"Modeling::Instances::create_instances":1,"Modeling::Points::scale_points":1,"Modeling::Points::randomize_point_rotation":1,"Modeling::Points::rotate_points":1,"Value::bool":1,"Modeling::Points::randomize_point_scale":1,"shaded_instances":1,"Core::Fields::fractal_noise_field":1,"Modeling::Points::displace_points":1,"Core::Graph::terminal":1,"color_array":1,"Core::Math::add":1,"Core::Randomization::fractal_noise":1,"Geometry::Properties::get_point_position":1,"Geometry::Mesh::update_mesh_normals":1,"Core::Conversion::vector3_to_scalar":1,"Core::Math::get_from_interpolated_array":1,"Core::FCurve::evaluate_fcurve":1,"fit_array_to_range":1,"Core::Array::build_array":1,"Core::Array::array_bounds":1,"